# Project vocabulary for the Smashing / Infinite Stream demo.
#
#   pattern<TAB>replacement      (regex; blank lines and # comments ignored)
#
# These splice in AFTER pronounce.py's BASE_SPEECH and BEFORE the de-pluralising
# tail, which is the detail that decides how the rung rules below are written.
# BASE already contains  \b(\d+)p\b -> "\1 p", so by the time these run "2160p"
# has ALREADY become "2160 p". Matching "2160p" here would never fire. Match the
# spaced form.
#
# Why spell the rungs out at all: left as digits the engine reads "2160 p" as
# "two thousand one hundred and sixty p", which is not how anyone says a video
# resolution out loud. Every rung the ladder can produce is listed, because a
# missing one is silently read the long way and you find out in playback.
\b234 p\b	two thirty four p
\b360 p\b	three sixty p
\b396 p\b	three ninety six p
\b432 p\b	four thirty two p
\b540 p\b	five forty p
\b594 p\b	five ninety four p
\b720 p\b	seven twenty p
\b954 p\b	nine fifty four p
\b1080 p\b	ten eighty p
\b1440 p\b	fourteen forty p
\b1800 p\b	eighteen hundred p
\b2160 p\b	twenty one sixty p

# Player and protocol vocabulary.
\bAVPlayer\b	A V player
\bABR\b	A B R
\bLL-DASH\b	L L DASH
\bDASH\b	dash
\bTTFF\b	time to first frame
\bRTT\b	R T T

# Device identifiers arrive from the wire as Apple's internal model string.
# "iPhone15,4" reads as "iPhone fifteen comma four" untouched. Deliberately
# mapped to the family rather than a marketing name — the record only tells us
# the family, and inventing "Plus" or "Pro" would be narration asserting
# something the data does not say.
iPhone(\d+),(\d+)	iPhone \1

# Infrastructure names that appear when narrating where a number came from.
\bnftables\b	N F tables
\bgo-proxy\b	go proxy
\bgo-live\b	go live
